Every final verdict has no open items on the charter or the documents. After all products were published, each KB auditor did a second pass on the other products' figures and seeded records. That pass found two real gaps, both now fixed: SSL had .ca renewal dates 5 days off, and the account deposit counted email and certificate renewals differently from how those products bill them.

Before delivery I checked the merge myself:
- The blueprint gates are green and the blueprint files are byte-identical to the version frozen before the difficulties started. The base was never edited.
- No business identifier repeats across difficulties: names, emails, phones, PINs, card digits, domains, order numbers and record ids all differ (492 values checked).
- In every difficulty all seven instantiation gates pass and the traceability check is clean.
- All 7 products' documents pass their check (domain 81, hosting 47, ssl 60, email 53, account 69, server 19, security 13).

Decisions and accepted residue, all logged in `worklog.md`:
- **Base defects left as they are** (marked BLUEPRINT-KNOWN, none moves a scored value):
  - The base hosting upgrade rule discounts upgrades by account tier, but the account rules say a tier discount never applies to prorated charges.
  - The base security product has conflicting refund and cleanup clauses, and the server and ssl products have a few loose parameter descriptions. They were published word for word.
  - The difficulty summaries say "the agent bills", while the skill requires one customer-made payment per task.
- **Customer-made payments:** I ruled that a customer-run write is acceptable when only the account holder could do it on the spot. That covers a payment they complete at checkout with a card or PayPal not on file (support staff never take card numbers), their own cancellation, or their own deposit. Each policy says so in its rule, and every auditor accepted it.
- **One price per certificate renewal:** my first ruling on how certificate auto-renewals are funded put two prices on the same renewal. I replaced it: the price is the same whatever pays, and account credit pays first when it covers that amount. As a result, the account task's deposit amounts changed.
- **Email took 16 rounds** because its product rules are thin for the required number of pricing factors. I refused to redraw the counts or bring back factors the auditor had rejected as unrealistic. It closed once I pointed to levers already recorded in the base: a downgrade scheduled for the renewal, and the rule that a full mailbox stops sending.
- **Rounding:** a computed half-up rounding can still miss an exact half-cent tie by a margin under $0.000000001. I accepted that residue as a known limit of the calculation language.

I also saved a memory note with this run's lessons for future scenarios. The coordinator scripts are in `/tmp/erl0076_coord/`, and the audit views under `.audit_views/` are kept because the reports cite them by hash.
